o solve this problem, we start by using the relationship between the coefficients of the polynomial and its roots given by Vieta's formulas. If the roots of the polynomial \\(x^2 - mx + n\\) are \\(p\\) and \\(q\\), where \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are positive prime integers, then we have:\n\\[ m = p + q \\]\n\\[ n = pq \\]\n\nWe are given the condition \\(m < 20\\), which translates to:\n\\[ p + q < 20 \\]\n\nWe need to determine all possible values of \\(n = pq\\) under the given conditions. We will consider all pairs of prime numbers \\((p, q)\\) such that their sum is less than 20.\n\nThe prime numbers less than 20 are:\n\\[ 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19 \\]\n\nWe will now list all pairs \\((p, q)\\) such that \\(p + q < 20\\):\n\n- For \\(p = 2\\):\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &q = 2: &2 + 2 = 4 &< 20 &\\quad n = 2 \\times 2 = 4 \\\\\n  &q = 3: &2 + 3 = 5 &< 20 &\\quad n = 2 \\times 3 = 6 \\\\\n  &q = 5: &2 + 5 = 7 &< 20 &\\quad n = 2 \\times 5 = 10 \\\\\n  &q = 7: &2 + 7 = 9 &< 20 &\\quad n = 2 \\times 7 = 14 \\\\\n  &q = 11: &2 + 11 = 13 &< 20 &\\quad n = 2 \\times 11 = 22 \\\\\n  &q = 13: &2 + 13 = 15 &< 20 &\\quad n = 2 \\times 13 = 26 \\\\\n  &q = 17: &2 + 17 = 19 &< 20 &\\quad n = 2 \\times 17 = 34 \\\\\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n- For \\(p = 3\\):\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &q = 3: &3 + 3 = 6 &< 20 &\\quad n = 3 \\times 3 = 9 \\\\\n  &q = 5: &3 + 5 = 8 &< 20 &\\quad n = 3 \\times 5 = 15 \\\\\n  &q = 7: &3 + 7 = 10 &< 20 &\\quad n = 3 \\times 7 = 21 \\\\\n  &q = 11: &3 + 11 = 14 &< 20 &\\quad n = 3 \\times 11 = 33 \\\\\n  &q = 13: &3 + 13 = 16 &< 20 &\\quad n = 3 \\times 13 = 39 \\\\\n  &q = 17: &3 + 17 = 20 &\\not< 20 &\\quad \\text{(not included)} \\\\\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n- For \\(p = 5\\):\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &q = 5: &5 + 5 = 10 &< 20 &\\quad n = 5 \\times 5 = 25 \\\\\n  &q = 7: &5 + 7 = 12 &< 20 &\\quad n = 5 \\times 7 = 35 \\\\\n  &q = 11: &5 + 11 = 16 &< 20 &\\quad n = 5 \\times 11 = 55 \\\\\n  &q = 13: &5 + 13 = 18 &< 20 &\\quad n = 5 \\times 13 = 65 \\\\\n  &q = 17: &5 + 17 = 22 &\\not< 20 &\\quad \\text{(not included)} \\\\\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n- For \\(p = 7\\):\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &q = 7: &7 + 7 = 14 &< 20 &\\quad n = 7 \\times 7 = 49 \\\\\n  &q = 11: &7 + 11 = 18 &< 20 &\\quad n = 7 \\times 11 = 77 \\\\\n  &q = 13: &7 + 13 = 20 &\\not< 20 &\\quad \\text{(not included)} \\\\\n  &q = 17: &7 + 17 = 24 &\\not< 20 &\\quad \\text{(not included)} \\\\\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n- For \\(p = 11\\):\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &q = 11: &11 + 11 = 22 &\\not< 20 &\\quad \\text{(not included)} \\\\\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n- For \\(p = 13, 17, 19\\):\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &p + q \\geq 13 + 7 = 20 &\\quad \\text{(not included)} \\\\\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\nSummarizing all valid \\(n\\) values:\n\\[\n4, 6, 9, 10, 14, 15, 21, 22, 25, 26, 33, 34, 35, 39, 49, 55, 65, 77\n\\]\n\nCounting these distinct values, we find there are 18 possible values of \\(n\\).\n\nThus, the number of possible values of \\(n\\) is \\(\\boxed{18}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{18}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
